# CODEOWNERS — INACTIVE.
#
# Every rule below is commented out on purpose. Real teams and handles have not been
# decided (docs/OPEN-DECISIONS.md, D6), and inventing one would route reviews to an
# identity that does not exist or, worse, to a real person who never agreed to it.
# tools/validate/validate.sh fails if any active line in this file names an owner.
#
# Routing to activate once owners exist:
#
#   /Contracts/                    <contract-owner> <ios-owner> <android-owner> <security-owner>
#   /tools/contract-codegen/       <contract-owner> <ios-owner> <android-owner> <security-owner>
#   /Sources/                      <ios-owner>
#   /Tests/                        <ios-owner>
#   /Package.swift                 <ios-owner>
#   /examples/ios-swiftui/         <ios-owner>
#   /android/                      <android-owner>
#   /settings.gradle.kts           <android-owner>
#   /build.gradle.kts              <android-owner>
#   /gradle/                       <android-owner>
#   /examples/android-compose/     <android-owner>
#   /Sources/SPFNAuth/             <ios-owner> <security-owner>
#   /Sources/SPFNHybrid/           <ios-owner> <security-owner>
#   /android/spfn-auth/            <android-owner> <security-owner>
#   /android/spfn-hybrid/          <android-owner> <security-owner>
#   /.github/workflows/            <release-owner> <security-owner>
#   /VERSION                       <release-owner> <security-owner>
#   /RELEASE.md                    <release-owner> <security-owner>
#   /CODEOWNERS                    <release-owner> <security-owner>
#
# Review semantics to remember when activating: GitHub satisfies a code-owner
# requirement when ONE of several owners on a path approves. Where contract, auth or
# release changes must have both a platform owner AND a security owner, CODEOWNERS
# alone is not enough — a separate merge policy or check has to verify that an approval
# from each role exists, and stale approvals must be dismissed on new pushes.
